Urinary Tract Infection
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Dysuria, Urinary urgency/frequency, Suprapubic discomfort, Positive leukocyte esterase/nitrites

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-049
Reason for Visit: Severe, acute-onset burning urination, relentless frequency, and visible blood in the urine causing significant localized pain and distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a rapid onset of agonizing dysuria, suprapubic pain, and macroscopic haematuria, completely preventing sleep and causing extreme discomfort. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to perform immediate point-of-care diagnostics, rule out ascending pyelonephritis, and initiate targeted oral antibiotic therapy to arrest the infection and alleviate the severe symptoms before regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: It burns intensely every time I try to pee, I have to go every five minutes, and the bowl is turning pink with blood.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced abruptly 12 hours ago. The patient reports intense dysuria, characterized by a sharp, burning, and stinging sensation during micturition. This is accompanied by profound urinary urgency requiring immediate access to a toilet, and markedly increased urinary frequency with the passage of only minimal volumes. They describe a constant sensation of incomplete bladder emptying and a dull, aching suprapubic pain. Over the last four hours, the urine has become cloudy, foul-smelling, and exhibits frank macroscopic haematuria. They explicitly deny high fevers, rigors, or flank pain. Symptoms developed shortly after an episode of sexual intercourse without adequate post-coital hygiene.
Functional Status: Independent, but functionally bound to the bathroom due to the relentless urgency and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Acute distress regarding the visible blood; significant sleep disruption.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 | HR: 86 | Temp: 37.3C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, shifting constantly, holding the lower abdomen, but not appearing systemically toxic.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: Mild, localized suprapubic tenderness to deep palpation. Back: Complete absence of costovertebral angle tenderness bilaterally. Genitourinary: Point-of-care midstream urine dipstick reveals strongly positive leukocyte esterase and nitrites, with significant visible blood. Expected investigation results will confirm significant bacteriuria on formal midstream urine culture.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Urinary Tract Infection - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Uncomplicated Cystitis)
Differentials:

Acute Pyelonephritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the explicit absence of systemic fevers, rigors, and negative costovertebral angle tenderness, localizing the infection strictly to the lower tract.

Nephrolithiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of severe, colicky unilateral flank pain, and the strong presence of infective markers on the urinalysis.

Interstitial Cystitis - Ruled out/unlikely as the acute onset and floridly positive bacterial markers on dipstick point definitively to an active bacterial infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with textbook symptoms of an acute, uncomplicated lower urinary tract infection. The severe localized pain, urgency, and inflammatory haematuria are causing acute, unmanageable distress. An urgent after-hours physical examination was necessary to definitively rule out upper tract involvement (pyelonephritis), safely interpret the point-of-care diagnostics, and immediately initiate empirical antibiotic therapy. Delaying treatment until the following day would unacceptably prolong the severe burning pain and needlessly increase the risk of the infection ascending to the kidneys.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Commenced oral Trimethoprim 300mg daily for 3 days. Advised concurrent use of over-the-counter urinary alkalizers to reduce the acidity and burning sensation. Collected a formal midstream urine sample to be sent for microscopy, culture, and sensitivities to confirm the specific uropathogen.
Positive Behavioral Change: To mechanically flush bacteria from the lower tract and prevent recurrence, the patient is instructed to establish a strict routine of urinating immediately after sexual intercourse, followed by drinking a large glass of water. Experiencing a sustained period without further painful infections will act as strong positive reinforcement, cementing this hygienic habit into their long-term routine.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 3 days to review the formal urine culture sensitivities and ensure complete symptom resolution.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate medical review or Emergency Department presentation is required if the patient develops high fevers, uncontrollable shivering, severe back pain, or intractable vomiting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The antibiotics target the most common bladder infections, but the treatment may be adjusted depending on the final laboratory results.
Clinical Course: The sharp burning and relentless urgency should improve significantly within 24 to 48 hours of starting the antibiotics.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you suddenly spike a high fever, start shaking, or develop intense pain in your mid-back, the infection may have moved to your kidneys. You must seek urgent medical care immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Bacteria have entered your bladder, causing severe inflammation of the lining. This makes the bladder bleed slightly and spasm, causing the burning and constant urge to pee. The antibiotics will kill the bacteria, while the alkalizing powder will neutralize the stinging acid in your urine.
Actionable Steps: Complete the full 3-day course of antibiotics without skipping a dose. Drink an extra liter of water daily. Always urinate immediately after sexual activity.

